# exec: init's sealer, /svc/exec (docs/CR-PLAN.md, track CE1; the
# proposal is docs/M13-PLAN.md's "F1b notes"). A program on /data runs
# only as the owner approved it (`allow`: its line in /data/etc/allow and
# its page table in /data/etc/allow.d, <dataprog.h>), from a VMO sealed
# against that table (vmo_make_exec_verified, which takes RIGHT_ROOT_VMEX:
# init's and the shell's alone), so that the kernel checks every page
# against the approval as it arrives. The shell seals the programs typed at
# it; this is the same for a program that another program starts
# (posix_spawn of a /data path: libc's file_program, user/posix/sys/spawn.c).
#
# The child's authority is its own approval only (the owner's decision,
# 2026-10-12): the namespace and the root powers its approved list
# (<wants.h>) asks for, resolved in init's namespace as the shell resolves
# them, whatever the caller holds. A caller with less may start an approved
# program with more; a caller with more gives it nothing of its own. So
# init starts the child itself, and the caller never holds anything the
# child is given: not its namespace, not its root resource, not a process
# handle that could make threads in it.
#
# Who serves it: init (user/services/init/exec.c), on a thread of its own
# (a slow filesystem never holds up init's loop), a channel per opener
# (svc.connect on the shared channel /svc/exec names). Who calls it: a
# program whose list asks for `svc exec` (libc's posix_spawn, utest,
# exectest); a program without it gets EACCES from posix_spawn of a /data
# path, as before.
#
# Reconnect: init never restarts, so an opener's channel closes only when
# the opener breaks the protocol (an answer bound left unread) or init
# shuts down. A sealed program (exec_prog below) is its own channel: it
# lasts until it is started, dropped, or left waiting past WAIT_NS.

protocol exec 47

# Openers at once (svc.connect's ERR_NO_RESOURCES past them), programs
# sealed and waiting for their start at once (open's ERR_NO_RESOURCES past
# them: all of them, and per opener), and how long one may wait before init
# drops it (its channel closed).
const OPENERS_MAX 32
const PROGRAMS_MAX 16
const PROGRAMS_PER_OPENER 4
const WAIT_NS 10000000000
# The most bytes of argv and the environment together (start's strings),
# and the most strings (more: ERR_INVALID_ARGS; spawn itself takes up to
# 2 MiB since CS1, carrying what its startup message can't in a VMO).
const STRINGS_BYTES 12288
const STRINGS_MAX 128

# Seal the program at `path` for a start (exec_prog.start). `path` is the
# file's absolute path, cleaned ("/data/bin/x": no "//", ".", ".." or
# trailing '/'), and `file` the caller's own open of it (a `file` channel,
# FS_READ: its namespace and views decide what it can name). init checks
# that `file` is the file its own /data has at `path` (the same id and
# size), that a line of the approvals names `path` and that its page table
# is the table of a file of this size; maps `file` (file.map, FILE_MAP_EXEC:
# a VMO only init holds) and seals it with the table, then reads the
# program's list from the sealed VMO (vmo_read: a page that doesn't match is
# refused here). `handover` is an empty channel end (the caller keeps the
# other): before start the caller writes the POSIX hand-over (<posixfds.h>)
# on its end, and init moves it onto the child's startup channel. Both are
# init's whatever the answer.
#   program  the sealed program: an `exec_prog` channel
#   posix    1 if its list says `posix`
#   wants    its list as the owner reads it ("exec, /data (read)"), "" if
#            it asks for nothing
# ERR_ACCESS_DENIED: not allowed (no line for the path, no page table: an
#   approval from before tables, run once from the shell), changed since it
#   was (another size, or a page of its headers or list not the approved
#   one), its list asks for what no program from /data may have, `file`
#   isn't the file at `path`, or a /data with no pages to give;
# ERR_WRONG_TYPE: not a program spawn can load (no ELF file, a broken list);
# ERR_BUSY: the file is open for writing; ERR_NOT_FOUND: nothing at `path`;
# ERR_INVALID_ARGS: `path` not a cleaned path on /data, a handle of the
#   wrong kind; ERR_NO_RESOURCES: PROGRAMS_MAX (or PROGRAMS_PER_OPENER on
#   this channel) waiting; ERR_IO: /data couldn't read it.
1  open  (str[<=4095] path, handle file, handle handover) -> (handle program, u8 posix, str[<=1023] wants)

# exec_prog: one sealed program, init's end served by init, good for one
# start. The caller closing it drops the program (and the hand-over end).
#
# Reconnect: there is none. init closes the channel after the start's
# answer, or when the program has waited WAIT_NS.

protocol exec_prog 48

# Start it: a new process in a new job inside `job` (the caller's own, or
# one of its children: a job handle with RIGHT_WRITE), so that a kill of
# the caller's job takes it too. Its startup message has `argc` strings of
# argv then the environment from `strings` (each NUL-terminated, argc of
# them at least 1), its namespace and root powers as its approved list
# says (the exec protocol's comment), `signals` as SR_SIGNALS (the child's
# end of its signal channel, `signal` protocol: empty) and, for a POSIX
# program, an 8 MiB stack; then whatever the caller wrote on its end of
# open's `handover`, message for message (at most 128). `job` and
# `signals` are init's whatever the answer.
#
# `image` is the caller's own program, if it has one: its SR_SELF_IMAGE
# (a duplicate with RIGHT_READ and RIGHT_EXEC at least). It tells init the
# list the caller was approved with, so that a child whose list asks for
# nothing beyond it starts as Linux starts a program that isn't set-user-ID:
# AT_SECURE 0, its environment whole and, for a POSIX program, the caller's
# current directory when its own namespace has it (init's exec.c says how
# and why). Without one, or one init can't read as an approved program's,
# the child is marked AT_SECURE and starts in "/", as every child did
# before. Read and closed by init.
#   proc  the child's process: RIGHT_WAIT, RIGHT_INSPECT, RIGHT_DUPLICATE
#         and RIGHT_TRANSFER (its end and its exit code; no RIGHT_WRITE,
#         which would make threads in it)
#   job   its job: RIGHT_MANAGE (job_kill: posix's SIGKILL, and its
#         limits), RIGHT_WAIT, RIGHT_INSPECT, RIGHT_DUPLICATE and
#         RIGHT_TRANSFER (no RIGHT_WRITE: nothing else starts in it)
# ERR_INVALID_ARGS: the strings malformed (a string without its NUL, argc
#   0 or past the strings), a handle of the wrong kind, a job without
#   RIGHT_WRITE, a hand-over message too big to move; ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE:
#   argv and the environment don't fit (spawn's limits), or more
#   than 128 hand-over messages; ERR_ACCESS_DENIED: a page the start reads
#   (the program headers, a writable segment) isn't the approved one;
#   spawn's own errors (ERR_NO_MEMORY, ERR_NO_RESOURCES: the job's limits).
1  start  (u16 argc, u8[<=12288] strings, handle job, handle signals, handle<vmo>[<=1] image) -> (handle proc, handle job)
